      <h1>Withdrawn: “JWT is not encryption”</h1>
      <div class="blog-post-meta">
        <span class="blog-post-date">Withdrawn August 29, 2026</span>
        <span class="blog-post-tag">correction</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="blog-article-lede">The original article has been removed because it presented a useful warning through inaccurate generalizations and unsupported first-person claims.</p>
    <div class="blog-body">
      <h2>What was wrong</h2>
      <p>A JSON Web Token is a token format, not one fixed protection mechanism. A JWT can be unsecured, integrity-protected with JWS, or encrypted with JWE. Saying that JWTs are categorically “signed, not encrypted” conflates related standards. Security also depends on algorithm and key selection, validation, storage, transport, expiry, revocation, and the surrounding application.</p>
      <p>The article also described review experience and incident patterns as personal observations without evidence I can publish. Examples and historical vulnerability claims were presented too categorically for a page that did not cite the primary standards and advisories.</p>
      <h2>What would be required to republish it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Primary citations to the relevant JOSE standards and current library guidance.</li>
        <li>Tested examples tied to named library versions and explicit threat models.</li>
        <li>A clear separation between format properties, implementation mistakes, and application policy.</li>
        <li>No first-person claim unless I can support it with an attributable artifact.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Until that work is done, this correction is more useful and more honest than the original post.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</div>

